    "faq": {
      "type": "etl-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is there a national teaching licence in Canada?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Teaching is regulated by each province and territory. You apply to the one where you want to teach.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I teach in Canada with a teaching qualification from another country?",
            "answer": "<p>Often, yes, but your qualification has to be assessed first. Regulators look at your degree, the length and content of your teacher-education programme, your practicum and your professional standing. Some receive full certification; others receive an interim or conditional certificate with courses or conditions to complete.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do I need Pathways to Teach Canada?",
            "answer": "<p>Only if you plan to teach in a participating province or territory: British Columbia, Manitoba, New Brunswick, Newfoundland and Labrador, the Northwest Territories, Nova Scotia, Nunavut, Prince Edward Island or Saskatchewan. Ontario, Alberta, Quebec and Yukon have their own processes.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the LCATP?",
            "answer": "<p>The Language Competency Assessment for the Teaching Profession is the language test run through Pathways to Teach Canada. It tests reading, writing, listening and speaking online. Ontario also accepts it.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I apply while I'm still outside Canada?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Pathways is online, and regulators such as Alberta publish a fee for applicants outside Canada. Starting before you move is one of the best ways to save time. I requested my documents and followed up with organisations from Qatar.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Does a work permit or permanent residence give me a teaching certificate?",
            "answer": "<p>No. Immigration status and teacher certification are separate. You need both to teach in a Canadian public school, and some provinces need your status before they'll issue a full certificate.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I apply for teaching jobs while my certification is in progress?",
            "answer": "<p>Sometimes. Some employers ask for certification before you apply; others will consider you if your assessment is underway. Read each vacancy carefully and describe your certification status accurately.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "If I'm certified in one province, can I move to another?",
            "answer": "<p>Usually. Under the Canadian Free Trade Agreement, a certified teacher should be recognised by another province or territory without significant extra requirements, though you will still file a transfer application and may need to show language proficiency.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q9": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is WES the same as teacher certification?",
            "answer": "<p>No. In Pathways provinces, WES completes the credential assessment, but the provincial regulator makes the certification decision.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
